WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.013 --> 00:00:04.000
Speaker 1: Let's go to Christian&nbsp;
from California. Christian, welcome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.720
Christian from California. Welcome.
Speaker 2:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.240
Can you hear me okay?
Speaker 1: Yes, I can.&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.800
Speaker 2: So I'm actually calling you from&nbsp;
the campus of my high school right now. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.400
I'm just wondering, what exactly do you think&nbsp;
is, you know, how Biden just forgave some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.880
student loan debt? What do you see as like&nbsp;
the long term solution to like how college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.400
is becoming more and more unaffordable?
Speaker 1: The long term solution is it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.640
not going to be just forgiving some debt. The long&nbsp;
term solution is going to be something has to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:44.560
some combination of downward pressure on the&nbsp;
educational costs and upward pressure on wages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:53.680
so that they are in line in a way that makes a lot&nbsp;
more sense. And unfortunately, I don't really see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.360
a clear path to that right now. I do think free&nbsp;
community college for two years for an associate's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:07.120
degree would do some of it. So if people had the&nbsp;
option of paying for four years at an expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:13.760
school or doing your first two years for free at&nbsp;
community college and then only doing two years at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.600
the expense of four year school, your junior and&nbsp;
senior year to finish out your four year degree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.720
I think that that would put significant&nbsp;
downward pressure on what the four year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.720
schools are able to charge, for example. I know&nbsp;
this one isn't popular with a lot of people, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.800
if it were a little more difficult to just get&nbsp;
a student loan to cover your entire education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:41.760
now, by more difficult, I mean, maybe it&nbsp;
has to be tied to something that makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.840
it more in line with the expected wages&nbsp;
for that degree or so. I don't know. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.040
difficult that might put downward pressure on&nbsp;
education costs, but it's very difficult. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:56.880
don't believe the solution is for giving a little&nbsp;
bit of debt here and there or even forgiving all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.360
of it, because those are basically licenses for&nbsp;
colleges to charge whatever the hell they want.&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.840
Speaker 2: What was it always sort of this&nbsp;
easy? I mean, like I don't know how long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.160
federal student loans have been around, but how&nbsp;
have they always been so accessible to just about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.320
anyone who wants it? I mean, sign up for one.
Speaker 1: No, but they also weren't as in demand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.520
because there was a time during which you could&nbsp;
kind of like work your way through college.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.120
The idea of that is kind of&nbsp;
absurd at this point in time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:30.880
So they've become more accessible as they've&nbsp;
become more in demand. So it's been both.&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.320
Speaker 2: Right. I have a patient at a clinic&nbsp;
I work at who says she went to Berkeley in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.840
sixties and was able to pay for it working&nbsp;
part time, which is insane to think of now.&nbsp;

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.880
Speaker 1: Thom Hartmann, who's a talk radio host&nbsp;
on the left, he talks about how his wife, Louise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.200
I believe it's that she was able to basically&nbsp;
waitress through college and pay for it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:57.600
come out with I think it's almost no debt.
Speaker 2: Wow. That's that's crazy to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.840
about. All right, David, thank you so&nbsp;
much for your time and taking my call.&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.680
Speaker 1: My pleasure. Thank you so much for the&nbsp;
question. Yeah, it's a lesson that the student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.640
loan debt forgiveness alone is not the solution&nbsp;
to the problem. That's that's for damn sure

